#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
Step 1: Create an ServiceAccount
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
#---------------------#
* Under RBAC directory
#---------------------#
1. Create an YAML file for service-account (sudo vi service-account.yml)
2. Config is in service-account.yml
3. kubectl apply -f service-account.yml
4. kubectl get sa
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
Step 2: Create an Secrets(tokens)
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
#---------------------#
* Under RBAC directory
#---------------------#
1. Create an YAML file for tokens[secrets] (sudo vi service-account-tokens.yml)
2. Config is in service-account-tokens.yml
3. kubectl apply -f service-account-tokens.yml
4. kubectl get secrets
5. kubectl describe secrets service-account-admin-token
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
Step 3: Create an Roles, RoleBinding, ClusterRole, ClusterRoleBinding
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
Note:
-----
(*) We, have predefined roles in AWS
(*) But, we don't have predefined roles in K8s we have to define our roles
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
#---------------------#
* Under RBAC directory
#---------------------#
1. Create an YAML file for Roles (sudo vi role.yml)
2. Config is in role.yml
3. Create an YAML file for Role-Binding (sudo vi role-binding.yml)
4. Config is in role-binding.yml
5. kubectl apply -f role.yml
6. kubectl apply -f role-binding.yml
7. kubectl get role
8. kubectl describe role pod-deployment-reader
9. kubectl get rolebinding
10. kubectl describe rolebinding role-bind-pod-deployment-reader
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
#---------------------#
* Under RBAC directory
#---------------------#
1. Create an YAML file for ClusterRole (sudo vi clusterrole.yml)
2. Config is in clusterrole.yml
3. kubectl apply -f clusterrole.yml
4. kubectl get clusterrole
5. kubectl describe clusterrole pod-deployment-admin
6. Create an YAML file for ClusterRole-Binding (sudo vi clusterrole-binding.yml)
7. Config is in clusterrole-binding.yml
8. kubectl apply -f clusterrole-binding.yml
9. kubectl get clusterrolebinding
10. kubectl describe clusterrolebinding role-bind-pod-deployment-reader
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
#-----------------------------------------------------#
* To connect to a Cluster, we need to create a Context
#-----------------------------------------------------#
Run the follwing command in local machine:
1. kubectl config set-context <context-name> --cluster=<cluster-name> --user=<user-name>

i.e.: kubectl config set-context dec-2025-readonly --cluster=dec-2025 --user=service-account-readonly
i.e.: kubectl config set-context dec-2025-admin --cluster=dec-2025 --user=service-account-admin
#---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------#
#-----------------------------------------------------#
* To switch between a Context
#-----------------------------------------------------#
Run the following command in local machine:
1. kubectl config use-context <context-name>

i.e.: kubectl config use-context dec-2025-readonly
i.e.: kubectl config use-context dec-2025-admin

2. Instead of using the above big command to switch between a context, use the following command:
kubectx <context-name>

i.e.: kubectx dec-2025-admin
i.e.: kubectx dec-2025-readonly
